# Lesson 08: Data Types in C# (Value Types, Reference Types & Memory Architecture)

In C#, every variable, constant, and expression has a type[cite: 30]. The type tells the compiler how much memory to allocate, the minimum and maximum values it can represent, and what operations are valid on that data[cite: 30].

Under the Common Type System (CTS), all data types in C# inherit from the root base type `System.Object` and are strictly partitioned into two categories: **Value Types** and **Reference Types**[cite: 10, 30].

---

## 1. Value Types vs. Reference Types: Memory Layout

The fundamental distinction lies in **what** the variable holds and **where** it lives in RAM:

* **Value Types:** The variable holds the **actual data directly**. They are allocated inline on the **Stack** (or within the object instance that encloses them). When copied, a duplicate of the value is created.
* **Reference Types:** The variable holds a **memory address (pointer)** on the Stack that references the actual object residing on the **Managed Heap**[cite: 10]. When copied, only the memory reference pointer is duplicated, pointing to the exact same heap memory.
