## 2. Complete Value Types Breakdown

Value types derive from `System.ValueType` (which derives from `System.Object`).

### A. Integral Types (Whole Numbers)
| C# Keyword | CTS Type | Memory Size | Signed? | Min / Max Value Range |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `sbyte` | `System.SByte` | 1 Byte (8 bits) | Yes | `-128` to `127` |
| `byte` | `System.Byte` | 1 Byte (8 bits) | No | `0` to `255` |
| `short` | `System.Int16` | 2 Bytes (16 bits) | Yes | `-32,768` to `32,767` |
| `ushort` | `System.UInt16` | 2 Bytes (16 bits) | No | `0` to `65,535` |
| `int` | `System.Int32` | 4 Bytes (32 bits) | Yes | `-2,147,483,648` to `2,147,483,647` |
| `uint` | `System.UInt32` | 4 Bytes (32 bits) | No | `0` to `4,294,967,295` |
| `long` | `System.Int64` | 8 Bytes (64 bits) | Yes | `-9.22 × 10^18` to `9.22 × 10^18` |
| `ulong` | `System.UInt64` | 8 Bytes (64 bits) | No | `0` to `18.44 × 10^18` |

### B. Floating-Point & High-Precision Types
| C# Keyword | CTS Type | Memory Size | Precision | Literal Suffix | Best Used For |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `float` | `System.Single` | 4 Bytes | ~6–9 digits | `f` or `F` (e.g., `3.14f`) | Graphics, gaming engines |
| `double` | `System.Double` | 8 Bytes | ~15–17 digits | None or `d` (default) | Scientific computations |
| `decimal` | `System.Decimal` | 16 Bytes | 28–29 digits | `m` or `M` (e.g., `99.99m`) | Financial math, money, tax |

### C. Boolean & Character Types
* **`bool` (`System.Boolean`):** 1 Byte. Stores `true` or `false`[cite: 30]. Unlike C++, integers (`0` or `1`) cannot be implicitly evaluated as booleans.
* **`char` (`System.Char`):** 2 Bytes (16-bit). Stores a single Unicode character represented in single quotes (`'A'`, `'\u0041'`)[cite: 30].

### D. User-Defined Value Types
* **`struct`:** Custom lightweight composite value type.
* **`enum`:** Strongly typed set of named integral constants.

