## 3. Reference Types Breakdown

Reference types inherit directly from `System.Object`.

### Built-in Reference Types
* **`object` (`System.Object`):** The ultimate base class for all .NET types[cite: 10]. Any value or reference type can be assigned to an object.
* **`string` (`System.String`):** A sequence of Unicode characters[cite: 30]. 
  * *Crucial Note:* Strings are **immutable**. Any operation that appears to alter a string creates a new string object on the heap.
* **`dynamic`:** Bypasses compile-time type verification; member lookups resolve dynamically at runtime.

### User-Defined Reference Types
* **`class`:** The fundamental OOP blueprint for objects.
* **`interface`:** Defines a behavioral contract that classes or structs implement.
* **`delegate`:** A type-safe function pointer referencing methods.
* **`record` (class record):** Immutable reference types featuring built-in value-based equality.
* **Arrays (`int[]`, `string[]`):** All arrays in C# are reference types allocated on the Managed Heap, even if their elements are value types.

